---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'docsindex' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Documentation"
  description="Three paths: trying it out, setting it up for a repository, deploying it for an organization.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b></p>
          <h1>Documentation</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Three paths. Pick the one that matches where you are, not the order the menu happens to be in.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm trying it out</h2>
          <p>You want to see what it does, on your own pull requests, without asking anyone's permission.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('install', 'en')}><span class="pn">Install the extension</span><span class="pd">From your browser's store, or from a release zip if your machine can't build anything. Five minutes.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('labels', 'en')}><span class="pn">Labels &amp; decorations</span><span class="pd">The thirteen labels, what each one says, and the rule that decides whether a comment blocks.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm setting it up for a repository</h2>
          <p>The team is convinced and you want everyone on the same rules.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('configure', 'en')}><span class="pn">Configure</span><span class="pd">One file on the default branch. Which keys matter, which ones bite, and how three levels of configuration combine.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('adoption', 'en')}><span class="pn">Adoption</span><span class="pd">assist → warn → enforce. What each step buys you, and how to get back out in minutes.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm deploying it for the organization</h2>
          <p>You want the convention to hold without depending on who installed what.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('server', 'en')}><span class="pn">The server</span><span class="pd">Docker, environment variables, storage, wiring the platforms, and making the check required.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('troubleshooting', 'en')}><span class="pn">Troubleshooting</span><span class="pd">The banners, the neutral statuses, and the two or three things that surprise everyone once.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Reference</h2>
          <p>Machine-readable, and stable.</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>The configuration schema</strong> at <code>conventionalcomments.io/schema/v1.json</code> — put it in your file's <code>$schema</code> and your editor completes the keys and underlines mistakes.</li>
            <li><strong>The convention itself</strong> lives at <a href="https://conventionalcomments.org/">conventionalcomments.org</a>. This site is tooling for it, and doesn't speak for it.</li>
            <li><strong>The source</strong>, Apache-2.0, with the normative specification and every acceptance criterion.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
      </div>
</Docs>
